theory RHTNCeremony
/*
================================================================================
  RHTN presence ceremony -- symbolic analysis (Tamarin)
================================================================================

WHAT THIS FILE IS
  The wire half of the presence ceremony (design Section 7-8, `wire-format.md`
  Section 4.5): a record a third party receives, and what that party can
  conclude from it alone.

  WHAT IT PROVES: ATTRIBUTABILITY.  An accepted record was signed, over that
  exact body, by every party the record names -- or that party's key was
  stolen.

  WHAT IT DOES NOT PROVE, AND WHY NOT.  Not that anybody met.  design Section
  7 says "bilateral collusion is unpreventable" and Section 15.4 that proof of
  presence "is not an unforgeable primitive, and bilateral collusion defeats
  it regardless".  Two people with their own uncompromised keys can sign a
  record for a meeting that never happened, and no validator can tell.  So
  this theory carries `Participant_Sign_Without_Meeting`, and any property
  resting on client conformity falsifies here by construction.

  The co-presence properties live in `compliant/ceremony.spthy`, where the
  signing discipline they assume is stated as the participant obligation it
  is, and where the collusion trace is kept reachable.

  WHY ATTRIBUTABILITY IS THE USEFUL THING.  design Section 15.4 treats
  presence as "an attack cost rather than an absolute primitive".  The cost
  lands because a fabricated record is a SIGNED LIE BY NAMED PARTIES rather
  than an anonymous forgery, and because no single signature can be
  transplanted onto another roster.  That is what a validator gets, and it is
  what this file establishes.

  Reading conventions (rule shape, sorts, trace, temporal logic) are in
  attach.spthy; new idioms are explained where they first appear.
================================================================================
*/

begin

builtins: signing, hashing

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  IDENTITIES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Participants and witnesses are ordinary nodes with long-term signing keys.
  A single Register rule serves all roles -- role is a matter of what a node
  DOES in a ceremony, not a separate kind of identity (design Section 3).
*/

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  IDENTITIES ARE HYBRID, AND THE TWO HALVES FAIL SEPARATELY
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  `wire-format.md`: a presence record is archive-retained, and the protocol
  "requires both identity components to sign an archive-retained transaction"
  -- one Ed25519 and one ML-DSA-65 signature over the same body, per logical
  signer.  The keyhash commits to the pair (Section 2.2).

  An earlier version of this theory carried ONE symbolic key and ONE
  `Compromised` event per party, which could express only "the whole signer is
  intact" or "the whole signer is gone".  The state the hybrid construction
  exists to survive -- ONE COMPONENT BROKEN, THE OTHER SOUND -- had no
  representation, so the theory could not distinguish a validator that checks
  both signatures from one that checks only the classical entry.  That is the
  property two components are there to buy, and it was the one thing the model
  could not see.

  Both halves are now separate keys with separate compromise events, and
  acceptance verifies both signatures.
*/

rule Register:
    [ Fr(~skc), Fr(~skp) ]
  --[ Reg($A), OnlyOnce($A) ]->
    [ !LtkC($A, ~skc), !LtkP($A, ~skp)
    , !Kh($A, h(<pk(~skc), pk(~skp)>))  // the identity IS the keyhash (§2.2)
    , Out(<pk(~skc), pk(~skp)>) ]      // KeyMaterial is public

rule Compromise_Classical:
    [ !LtkC($A, ~skc) ]
  --[ Compromised($A), CompromisedClassical($A) ]->
    [ Out(~skc) ]

rule Compromise_PostQuantum:
    [ !LtkP($A, ~skp) ]
  --[ Compromised($A), CompromisedPQ($A) ]->
    [ Out(~skp) ]

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  THE CO-PRESENCE AXIOM
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  A ceremony is identified by a fresh ceremony token ~cid (design Section
  7.4.1's pre-commitment plays this role: a unique per-ceremony value).
  The Meet rule mints CoPresent facts for the two participants and one
  witness, all sharing the same ~cid, and stamps a MeetHappened action
  recording exactly WHO was physically together.

  A CoPresent fact is LINEAR and each is CONSUMED by the party using it to
  sign, so a single meeting authorises a single ceremony's signatures --
  the adversary cannot recycle one co-presence token across many forged
  records.

  The restriction DistinctParties forbids the degenerate "meeting" of a
  party with itself, mirroring the design's rule that participants and
  witnesses are distinct roles (wire Section 3.2: a participant is not its
  own witness; the two participants are distinct).
*/

rule Meet:
    [ Fr(~cid) ]                       // a fresh ceremony identifier
  --[ MeetHappened($P1, $P2, $W, ~cid) // trace: who was physically present
    , DistinctParties($P1, $P2, $W) ]->
    [ CoPresent($P1, ~cid)             // the two PARTICIPANTS were present
    , CoPresent($P2, ~cid)
    , !Ceremony($P1, $P2, $W, ~cid)    // persistent record of the roster
    // THE CEREMONY ID IS PUBLIC.  A presence record is published -- witnesses
    // are handed it to notarise, evaluators fetch it (§15's attestation
    // class), and nothing anywhere treats the id as a secret.  Withholding it
    // made the honest path UNREACHABLE: Witness_Sign and Accept_Record take
    // the body from the network, and the network could not construct a body
    // containing a fresh value nobody published.  Every lemma still verified,
    // through traces in which the participants' keys were stolen and the
    // adversary chose its own id -- so six green lemmas said nothing about an
    // honest ceremony.  See the two honest-completion guards below.
    , Out(~cid) ]

/*
  THE WITNESS GETS NO CO-PRESENCE TOKEN, and this is the design's rule, not
  a simplification.  design Section 7.6: "Witnesses notarise; they do not
  verify proximity ... the co-presence evidence that matters is generated
  between the two devices and merely reported to witnesses" -- and that
  section goes on to say "the record format must not imply otherwise".  An
  earlier version of this model gave the witness a CoPresent token and made
  Witness_Sign consume it, which required the notary to attend the meeting
  and so excluded ordinary specified executions.  All four lemmas below
  verify without it; what the theorem is about is the PARTICIPANTS'
  co-presence, which is what the design claims.
*/

restriction DistinctParties:
  "All a b w #i. DistinctParties(a, b, w) @ #i
     ==> not(a = b) & not(a = w) & not(b = w)"

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  THE CEREMONY SIGNATURES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  A presence record binds: the two participants and the witness each sign
  the ceremony (design Section 8.1, wire Section 4.5 -- the envelope carries
  participant and witness signatures over the record body).  We model the
  record body as <'presence', P1, P2, W, cid>: the roster plus the ceremony
  id.  Each PARTICIPANT's rule CONSUMES its CoPresent token for that cid, so a
  participant signature can only be produced by a party that was actually
  present -- or by anyone holding that party's key, via Compromise.  The
  witness signs as a remote notary and consumes no token (see Meet).

  Each signer emits its signature to the network; the verifier (below)
  collects all three and checks them.
*/

// A participant signs the ceremony it attended.
rule Participant_Sign:
    [ !LtkC($P, ~skc), !LtkP($P, ~skp)
    , CoPresent($P, cid)               // consumed: this party was present
    , !Ceremony($P1, $P2, $W, cid) ]   // the roster this cid belongs to
  --[ ParticipantSigned($P, $P1, $P2, $W, cid)
    , HonestParticipantSigned($P, $P1, $P2, $W, cid)
    , SignedBody($P, $P1, $P2, $W, cid) ]->
    [ Out(<sign(<'rhtn/1:presence', <$P1, $P2, $W, cid>>, ~skc),
           sign(<'rhtn/1:presence', <$P1, $P2, $W, cid>>, ~skp)>) ]

// A witness signs the ceremony it observed.
/*
  A WITNESS NOTARISES WHAT IT IS SHOWN, and takes the roster FROM THE NETWORK
  rather than from a ground-truth fact.  design Section 7.6: "Witnesses
  notarise; they do not verify proximity ... the co-presence evidence that
  matters is generated between the two devices and merely reported to
  witnesses" -- and design Section 7.6 also says two colluding parties can
  simulate the whole exchange and NO WITNESS CAN TELL.

  An earlier version premised this rule on !Ceremony, which only the physical
  Meet rule creates, so an honest witness could sign only a meeting that had
  really happened -- a remote notary given a truth oracle the design says it
  does not have.  Removing it costs the theorems nothing, because what carries
  them is the PARTICIPANTS' co-presence tokens, and gains honesty: a witness
  here will notarise a fabricated roster exactly as the design says it would.
*/
/*  A LEGITIMATE PRINCIPAL SIGNING A BODY IT NEVER MET FOR.
    ================================================================
    Its own key, no `CoPresent` token, no compromise, and the roster taken
    from the network -- which is what two parties agreeing a fake meeting
    remotely actually looks like.

    THIS RULE IS WHY THE THEORY IS HONEST ABOUT ITS TREE.  design 7 says
    "bilateral collusion is unpreventable", and design 15.4 that proof of
    presence "is not an unforgeable primitive, and bilateral collusion
    defeats it regardless".  A wire theory that forbids a key owner from
    signing what it likes is assuming CLIENT CONFORMITY, and conformity is
    not something a validator can check.

    Adding this rule falsified four lemmas that used to live here --
    presence_requires_copresence, no_remote_forgery,
    copresence_binds_one_roster and formation_requires_copresence.  They were
    not wrong; they were in the wrong tree, and they are now in
    `compliant/ceremony.spthy` where the discipline they assume is stated as
    the participant obligation it is.                                       */
rule Participant_Sign_Without_Meeting:
    [ !LtkC($P, ~skc), !LtkP($P, ~skp), In(<$P1, $P2, $W, cid>) ]
  --[ ParticipantSigned($P, $P1, $P2, $W, cid)
    , SignedWithoutMeeting($P)
    , SignedBody($P, $P1, $P2, $W, cid) ]->
    [ Out(<sign(<'rhtn/1:presence', <$P1, $P2, $W, cid>>, ~skc),
           sign(<'rhtn/1:presence', <$P1, $P2, $W, cid>>, ~skp)>) ]

rule Formation_Sign_Without_Meeting:
    [ !LtkC($P, ~skc), !LtkP($P, ~skp), In(<$P1, $P2, cid>) ]
  --[ FormationSigned($P, $P1, $P2, cid)
    , SignedWithoutMeeting($P)
    , SignedFormation($P, $P1, $P2, cid) ]->
    [ Out(<sign(<'rhtn/1:formation', <$P1, $P2, cid>>, ~skc),
           sign(<'rhtn/1:formation', <$P1, $P2, cid>>, ~skp)>) ]

rule Witness_Sign:
    [ !LtkC($W, ~skc), !LtkP($W, ~skp)
    , In(<'presence', $P1, $P2, $W, cid>) ]   // whatever it is handed
  --[ WitnessSigned($W, $P1, $P2, $W, cid)
    , SignedBody($W, $P1, $P2, $W, cid) ]->
    [ Out(<sign(<'rhtn/1:presence', <$P1, $P2, $W, cid>>, ~skc),
           sign(<'rhtn/1:presence', <$P1, $P2, $W, cid>>, ~skp)>) ]

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ACCEPTING A RECORD
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  A third party (a later evaluator) accepts a presence record for roster
  <P1,P2,W,cid> when it has all three binding signatures, each verifying
  against the named identity's public key.  This is wire Section 4.5's
  structural check reduced to its authenticating core: the record is only
  as good as the signatures over it, and every one must verify.
*/

rule Accept_Record:
    [ !Kh($P1, kh1), !Kh($P2, kh2), !Kh($W, khw)
    , In(<'presence', $P1, $P2, $W, cid>)   // the record body, as received
    , In(<km1, km2, kmw>)                   // and CANDIDATE KeyMaterial for
                                            //   each party the record names
    , In(<sig1c, sig1p>), In(<sig2c, sig2p>), In(<sigwc, sigwp>) ]
    /*  THE KEY MATERIAL IS BOUND BEFORE IT IS USED.  `wire-format.md`
        requires carried `KeyMaterial` to hash to the keyhash it purports to
        describe; without that, one identity can be presented with another
        party's keys.  Looking `!PkC`/`!PkP` up BY IDENTITY -- as this rule
        did until 2026-09-07 -- made the identity-to-key relation true before
        validation, so a validator omitting the check had no representation
        and the attribution lemmas stayed green for exactly that error.
        `attach.spthy` had already been corrected this way; these three
        theories had not.  Drop any of the three Eq(h(...)) below and
        `an_accepted_record_is_attributable` falsifies.                    */
    // BOTH entries per logical signer.  Drop any one of the six and
    // `an_accepted_record_is_attributable` falsifies -- which is true only
    // because its carve-out requires BOTH halves of a signer to be
    // compromised.  An earlier version carved out on `Compromised(A)`, which
    // either half emits, so dropping P2's or W's post-quantum check left
    // every lemma green.  Verified: that mutation now falsifies.
  --[ Eq(h(km1), kh1), Eq(h(km2), kh2), Eq(h(kmw), khw)
    , Eq(verify(sig1c, <'rhtn/1:presence', <$P1, $P2, $W, cid>>, fst(km1)), true)
    , Eq(verify(sig1p, <'rhtn/1:presence', <$P1, $P2, $W, cid>>, snd(km1)), true)
    , Eq(verify(sig2c, <'rhtn/1:presence', <$P1, $P2, $W, cid>>, fst(km2)), true)
    , Eq(verify(sig2p, <'rhtn/1:presence', <$P1, $P2, $W, cid>>, snd(km2)), true)
    , Eq(verify(sigwc, <'rhtn/1:presence', <$P1, $P2, $W, cid>>, fst(kmw)), true)
    , Eq(verify(sigwp, <'rhtn/1:presence', <$P1, $P2, $W, cid>>, snd(kmw)), true)
    // THE VALIDATOR CHECKS ROLE DISTINCTNESS ITSELF (`wire-format.md` §3.2:
    // a participant is not its own witness, the two participants are
    // distinct).  It was previously enforced only at Meet and reached this
    // rule transitively, through the !Ceremony premise Witness_Sign used to
    // carry.  Once a witness notarises what it is handed, that path is gone
    // and the degenerate roster P1 = P2 = W becomes assemblable from ONE
    // signature, which satisfies all three checks at once.  A real validator
    // rejects it structurally, so this rule does too.
    , DistinctParties($P1, $P2, $W)
    , RecordAccepted($P1, $P2, $W, cid) ]->
    [ ]


/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  FORMATION CEREMONIES -- the witnessless subtype
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  design Section 13.2 and `wire-format.md` Section 4.5 field 6: a FORMATION
  record "has empty witness and verifier arrays permanently, and must never
  age into looking like a normal record".  Two people meet and start a subnet
  with nobody else present, because there is nobody else yet.

  Modelling only the witnessed subtype -- which an earlier version of this
  theory did -- leaves the co-presence theorems silent about a structurally
  legal record.  The rules below add the branch: a formation meeting mints
  co-presence for the two participants and no witness, and a formation record
  is accepted on their two signatures alone.

  WHAT A FORMATION RECORD IS WORTH IS A SEPARATE QUESTION and not this
  theory's: design Section 13.2 makes it "evidence only to its two
  participants", which is a WEIGHT rule (design Section 16.1), not a
  structural one.  What is proved here is the same physical claim as for a
  normal record -- an accepted formation record implies its two participants
  met -- which is the most a signature check can say either way.
*/

rule Meet_Formation:
    [ Fr(~cid) ]
  --[ FormationMeetHappened($P1, $P2, ~cid)
    , DistinctPair($P1, $P2) ]->
    [ CoPresent($P1, ~cid)
    , CoPresent($P2, ~cid)
    , !Formation($P1, $P2, ~cid)
    , Out(~cid) ]                      // public, as above

restriction DistinctPair:
  "All a b #i. DistinctPair(a, b) @ #i ==> not(a = b)"

rule Formation_Sign:
    [ !LtkC($P, ~skc), !LtkP($P, ~skp)
    , CoPresent($P, cid)
    , !Formation($P1, $P2, cid) ]
  --[ FormationSigned($P, $P1, $P2, cid)
    , HonestFormationSigned($P, $P1, $P2, cid)
    , SignedFormation($P, $P1, $P2, cid) ]->
    [ Out(<sign(<'rhtn/1:formation', <$P1, $P2, cid>>, ~skc),
           sign(<'rhtn/1:formation', <$P1, $P2, cid>>, ~skp)>) ]

rule Accept_Formation:
    [ !Kh($P1, kh1), !Kh($P2, kh2)
    , In(<km1, km2>)                        // candidate KeyMaterial, bound below
    , In(<'formation', $P1, $P2, cid>)      // the record body, as received
    , In(<sig1c, sig1p>), In(<sig2c, sig2p>) ]
  --[ Eq(h(km1), kh1), Eq(h(km2), kh2)
    , Eq(verify(sig1c, <'rhtn/1:formation', <$P1, $P2, cid>>, fst(km1)), true)
    , Eq(verify(sig1p, <'rhtn/1:formation', <$P1, $P2, cid>>, snd(km1)), true)
    , Eq(verify(sig2c, <'rhtn/1:formation', <$P1, $P2, cid>>, fst(km2)), true)
    , Eq(verify(sig2p, <'rhtn/1:formation', <$P1, $P2, cid>>, snd(km2)), true)
    // THE VALIDATOR CHECKS PARTICIPANT DISTINCTNESS ITSELF, as Accept_Record
    // does (`wire-format.md` §3.2: the two participants are distinct).
    // Enforcing it only at Meet_Formation is not enough: this rule takes its
    // body and signatures from the NETWORK, so without the check a body with
    // P1 = P2 is acceptable here and one signature satisfies both slots.
    , DistinctPair($P1, $P2)
    , FormationAccepted($P1, $P2, cid) ]->
    [ ]

/*
  ONE REGISTRATION PER NAME.  A public label like $A stands for a keyhash,
  and design Section 5.1 makes a keyhash a commitment to the key material --
  change the material and you have a different identity.  Without the
  restriction below, Register could fire twice for one label and give a
  single symbolic name two unrelated keys, so "the party I meant to reach"
  would not pin a key.  (currency.spthy's Subject_Key is deliberately NOT
  restricted this way: a subject holding several keys over time is rotation,
  which is that theory's subject matter.)
*/
restriction OneKeyPerName:
  "All a #i #j. OnlyOnce(a) @ #i & OnlyOnce(a) @ #j ==> #i = #j"

restriction Equality:
  "All x y #i. Eq(x, y) @ #i ==> x = y"

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  LEMMAS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
*/

// SANITY: an honest ceremony produces an acceptable record.  Without this
// the security lemma could be vacuously true.  (This is the executability
// check the plan-adjacent notes flag as the first thing to write.)
lemma ceremony_is_executable:
  exists-trace
  "Ex P1 P2 W cid #i. RecordAccepted(P1, P2, W, cid) @ #i"

// THE GUARDS THAT ACTUALLY GUARD.  An honest ceremony, and an honest
// formation, reaching acceptance with NO compromise anywhere.  The
// executability lemmas above do not establish this and did not: both were
// satisfied by traces in which participant keys were stolen, so every
// universal lemma below could have held because uncompromised acceptance
// never occurred.  An exists-trace guard is only a guard if it excludes
// compromise.
/*  THESE MUST NAME THE CO-PRESENCE BRANCH, and an earlier version did not.
    Once `Participant_Sign_Without_Meeting` exists, "a meeting happened AND a
    record was accepted" can be satisfied with the honest rule never firing --
    the meeting and the signatures need not be connected.  Deleting
    `Participant_Sign` outright left this lemma VERIFYING, which is precisely
    the vacuity an anti-vacuity guard exists to catch.

    `HonestParticipantSigned` is emitted only by the rule that consumes a
    `CoPresent` token, so requiring it from BOTH participants pins the guard
    to the path it is meant to protect.                                     */
lemma honest_ceremony_completes:
  exists-trace
  "Ex P1 P2 W cid #m #a #b #i.
       MeetHappened(P1, P2, W, cid) @ #m
     & HonestParticipantSigned(P1, P1, P2, W, cid) @ #a
     & HonestParticipantSigned(P2, P1, P2, W, cid) @ #b
     & RecordAccepted(P1, P2, W, cid) @ #i
     & #m < #a & #m < #b & #a < #i & #b < #i
     & not(Ex A #k. Compromised(A) @ #k)"

lemma honest_formation_completes:
  exists-trace
  "Ex P1 P2 cid #m #a #b #i.
       FormationMeetHappened(P1, P2, cid) @ #m
     & HonestFormationSigned(P1, P1, P2, cid) @ #a
     & HonestFormationSigned(P2, P1, P2, cid) @ #b
     & FormationAccepted(P1, P2, cid) @ #i
     & #m < #a & #m < #b & #a < #i & #b < #i
     & not(Ex A #k. Compromised(A) @ #k)"

/*  WHAT A VALIDATOR CAN ACTUALLY CONCLUDE: ATTRIBUTABILITY.

    Not "these people met" -- the wire cannot deliver that, and the rule
    above is why.  What it delivers is that an accepted record is ATTRIBUTED
    correctly: each party the record names really did sign that exact body,
    or its key was stolen.  A colluding participant satisfies this, because
    it genuinely signed; that is the point, and it is what makes the record
    evidence against the signer rather than proof of a meeting.

    design 15.4 puts the same boundary in words -- presence is "an attack
    cost rather than an absolute primitive".  The cost lands because the
    record is attributable, so a fabricated one is a signed lie by named
    parties.  THAT is the wire property.

    MUTATION-TESTED, because a lemma this weak-sounding has to be shown to do
    work: delete the `verify(sig1, ...)` check from `Accept_Record` and this
    falsifies, while `an_accepted_formation_is_attributable` -- a different
    rule -- stays verified.  The mutation hits exactly the lemma it should
    and no other.                                                           */
lemma an_accepted_record_is_attributable:
  "All P1 P2 W cid #i.
     RecordAccepted(P1, P2, W, cid) @ #i
     ==> ( ( (Ex #a. SignedBody(P1, P1, P2, W, cid) @ #a & #a < #i)
           | ( (Ex #x. CompromisedClassical(P1) @ #x & #x < #i)
             & (Ex #y. CompromisedPQ(P1) @ #y & #y < #i) ) )
         & ( (Ex #b. SignedBody(P2, P1, P2, W, cid) @ #b & #b < #i)
           | ( (Ex #x. CompromisedClassical(P2) @ #x & #x < #i)
             & (Ex #y. CompromisedPQ(P2) @ #y & #y < #i) ) )
         & ( (Ex #w. SignedBody(W, P1, P2, W, cid) @ #w & #w < #i)
           | ( (Ex #x. CompromisedClassical(W) @ #x & #x < #i)
             & (Ex #y. CompromisedPQ(W) @ #y & #y < #i) ) ) )"


/*  WHAT "EVERY PARTY IT NAMES" MEANS HERE, stated because the lemma above is
    easy to read as more than it is.

    THE SIGNER SET IS ONE WITNESS.  `wire-format.md` permits a presence record
    up to 16 witnesses and 32 verifier responses; this theory carries a single
    `W`.  So the lemma covers every party the MODEL names, and a validator bug
    that verified witness 1 of 2 and skipped the rest has no representation
    here.

    THE BODY IS A PROJECTION.  The signed term is <'presence', P1, P2, W, cid>,
    where the wire body also carries timestamps, verifier responses, witness
    metadata and disclosure commitments, all of which the signature must
    cover.  Two distinct wire bodies agreeing on this projection map to one
    Tamarin term, so a signature-coverage error that distinguishes them is
    invisible to this theory.

    Neither is a defect in the specification, which requires the coverage and
    the full signer set.  Both are limits on what has been FORMALLY
    established, and no refinement argument has been supplied connecting this
    projection to arbitrary permitted wire bodies.                          */

lemma formation_is_executable:
  exists-trace
  "Ex P1 P2 cid #i. FormationAccepted(P1, P2, cid) @ #i"

/*  The formation counterpart, and the same boundary.  A formation record
    carries no witness (design 13.2), so attributability is the whole of what
    a validator gets -- which the design already says plainly, treating
    colluding creation as expected rather than impossible.                  */
lemma an_accepted_formation_is_attributable:
  "All P1 P2 cid #i.
     FormationAccepted(P1, P2, cid) @ #i
     ==> ( ( (Ex #a. SignedFormation(P1, P1, P2, cid) @ #a & #a < #i)
           | ( (Ex #x. CompromisedClassical(P1) @ #x & #x < #i)
             & (Ex #y. CompromisedPQ(P1) @ #y & #y < #i) ) )
         & ( (Ex #b. SignedFormation(P2, P1, P2, cid) @ #b & #b < #i)
           | ( (Ex #x. CompromisedClassical(P2) @ #x & #x < #i)
             & (Ex #y. CompromisedPQ(P2) @ #y & #y < #i) ) ) )"

end
